---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Dokploy deployment"
  path="/docs/self-hosting/dokploy"
  description="Run KoAkademy on Dokploy, the open-source Docker PaaS, using the repository's Compose file."
>
  <p>
    Dokploy is an open-source PaaS you run on your own server. It builds on
    Docker and Swarm, ships its own Traefik proxy with automatic Let's Encrypt
    certificates, and can back up named volumes to S3-compatible storage. This
    guide adapts KoAkademy's Compose topology to Dokploy's model — the
    application image itself is unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A Linux VPS running Ubuntu or Debian with at least 2 GB RAM and 30 GB disk (Dokploy's own recommendation)</li>
    <li>Ports <code>80</code>, <code>443</code>, and <code>3000</code> reachable from the internet</li>
    <li>A domain (for example <code>school.example</code>) pointing to the server</li>
    <li>A stable KoAkademy release tag and an external S3-compatible bucket</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Install Dokploy</h2>
  <p>Dokploy provides a one-line installer:</p>
  <pre><code>curl -sSL https://dokploy.com/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
  <p>
    When it finishes, open <code>https://&lt;server-ip&gt;:3000</code> and create
    the administrator account.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-callout doc-callout-caution">
    <p class="doc-callout-label">Running remote code is privileged</p>
    <p>
      The installer is a remote script executed as root. Download it first with
      <code>curl -fsSLO https://dokploy.com/install.sh</code>, read it with
      <code>less install.sh</code>, then run <code>bash install.sh</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Prepare the Compose source</h2>
  <p>
    KoAkademy ships a production Compose topology. Fetch it at the stable tag
    you intend to deploy:
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone --depth 1 --branch &lt;latest-stable-tag&gt; https://github.com/yukazakiri/koakademy.git
cd koakademy</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>compose.production.yaml</code> defines the application, PostgreSQL,
    Redis, and Gotenberg services. <code>.env.production.example</code> is the
    public contract for every variable. Three adaptations matter for Dokploy:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Keep PostgreSQL data on a <strong>named volume</strong> (for example
      <code>pg_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data</code>). Dokploy's automated volume
      backups only cover named volumes — bind mounts are excluded.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not publish host ports for the application. Dokploy routes traffic
      through Traefik to the container's port <code>8000</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pass credentials as plain environment variables in Dokploy's
      <code>.env</code>. Docker secrets and the Swarm installer's secret
      handling do not apply on this path.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because only Dokploy's Traefik can reach the application on the internal
    network, <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> is acceptable here — see
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/configuration">Configuration</a> for the
    warning behind that choice.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Create the Compose service</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In a project and environment, choose <strong>Create Service → Docker Compose</strong>.</li>
    <li>Paste the adapted <code>compose.production.yaml</code> into the editor, or point the service at your repository and the compose file path.</li>
    <li>
      In the <strong>Environment</strong> tab, add every value from
      <code>.env.production.example</code>: <code>APP_URL</code>,
      <code>PORTAL_HOST</code>, <code>ADMIN_HOST</code>, <code>APP_KEY</code>,
      <code>DB_*</code>, <code>REDIS_*</code>, <code>GOTENBERG_URL</code>,
      <code>AWS_*</code> storage credentials, <code>MAIL_*</code>, and
      <code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>. Dokploy writes these to the service's
      <code>.env</code>, which the compose file loads with <code>env_file</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leave <code>COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME</code> at its default — Dokploy relies on
      it to target the service containers.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Deploy the service, then continue with domains.</p>

  <h2>4. Domains and TLS</h2>
  <p>
    In the service's <strong>Domains</strong> tab, add your domain (for example
    <code>school.example</code>). Dokploy injects the Traefik routing labels and
    provisions a Let's Encrypt certificate automatically. If you use split
    portal/admin subdomains, add both hostnames and keep
    <code>PORTAL_HOST</code> and <code>ADMIN_HOST</code> in sync.
  </p>
  <p>
    Verify the application answers before the setup wizard:
    <code>curl --fail --silent --show-error https://school.example/up</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Run migrations and complete setup</h2>
  <p>
    KoAkademy keeps <code>RUN_MIGRATIONS=false</code>; schema changes are
    deliberate one-off operations. First set a stable application key: generate
    one once with <code>php artisan key:generate --show</code> (locally, or in
    the service terminal) and paste the complete value into <code>APP_KEY</code>
    in the Environment tab. A key written only to the container's
    <code>.env</code> would be lost on the next redeploy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then open the service's <strong>Terminal</strong> (or define a Dokploy
    schedule job) and run:
  </p>
  <pre><code>php artisan migrate --force</code></pre>
  <p>
    Visit <code>https://school.example/setup</code> to create the institution,
    initial academic period, and first administrator, then verify
    <code>/admin</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Backups</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Use Dokploy's <strong>Volume Backups</strong> feature to push the
      PostgreSQL named volume to an S3-compatible destination on a schedule.
      Enable the "turn off container during backup" option for a consistent
      snapshot.
    </li>
    <li>
      Back up the S3 bucket that holds uploads separately, with the storage
      provider's versioning and retention controls.
    </li>
    <li>
      Preserve <code>.env</code> and <code>APP_KEY</code> in an encrypted secret
      store — losing <code>APP_KEY</code> can make encrypted data and sessions
      unreadable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Upgrades</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the release notes and take a database backup.</li>
    <li>Update the image tag (<code>KOAKADEMY_VERSION</code>) to the new stable release and redeploy.</li>
    <li>Run <code>php artisan migrate --force</code> from the terminal before or immediately after the new container starts.</li>
    <li>Smoke-test the portal, <code>/admin</code>, uploads, a queued job, and a PDF export.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The full upgrade and rollback rules in
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/deployment">Deployment</a> apply unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the service logs and the general
    <a href="/docs/self-hosting/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> guide.
    Common Dokploy-specific causes: environment changes not followed by a
    redeploy, a missing or rotated <code>APP_KEY</code>, PostgreSQL volume not
    attached, and a domain added before <code>APP_URL</code> was updated.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
